 ## Overview
-The Ideal Low-Pass Filter (Rect Filter) is a theoretical reconstruction method that suppresses all spectral replicas except the baseband spectrum, allowing exact signal reconstruction if sampling criteria are met [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its siblings by its perfect frequency response in the passband and complete stopband attenuation, corresponding to a sinc function in the spatial domain [S1, unofficial]. Unlike practical approximations, it serves as the theoretical upper bound for interpolation quality, though it is seldom used in practice due to the infinite extent of its spatial kernel [S1, unofficial].
+The Ideal Low-Pass Filter (Rect Filter) is a theoretical reconstruction method that suppresses all spectral replicas except the baseband spectrum, allowing exact signal reconstruction if sampling criteria are met [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its siblings, such as [Frequency Domain Depth Cueing via Impulse Shifting](862-frequency-domain-depth-cueing-via-impulse-shifting.md) and [POCS Resampling Filter Design](6373-pocs-resampling-filter-design.md), by its perfect frequency response in the passband and complete stopband attenuation, corresponding to a sinc function in the spatial domain [S1, unofficial]. Unlike practical approximations, it serves as the theoretical upper bound for interpolation quality, though it is seldom used in practice due to the infinite extent of its spatial kernel [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A sampled signal spectrum $F_s(\nu)$ and the Nyquist rate.
-*   **Output:** The reconstructed baseband spectrum $F_r(\nu)$, which corresponds to the original continuous signal if properly sampled.
